raft dispatch. Higher terms trigger state/reset transitions (subject to the documented recent-leader vote suppression); lower-term messages are ignored. Reset clears votes, pending readIndex state, transfer state and replication progress, while log commitment persists. New leadership appends a current-term no-op and reconstructs pending configuration-change status from the log. Election is blocked while committed entries remain unapplied, conservatively covering pending membership changes. Connection: support is evaluated in the current term and voter set, and prior commitment constrains later history; pending read support is abandoned on reset rather than carried into a new authority context. This is partial understanding, not a ready core map: membership application and recovery/completion ownership still need tracing.\\n\\nConcrete source discrepancy retained for investigation: readIndex.confirm(ctx) returns every queued status through ctx, retaining each status's own ctx/from and assigning the confirming status's index; it removes all returned statuses from pending. handleReadIndexLeaderConfirmation uses s.ctx for local addReadyToRead, but its remote ReadIndexResp uses m.Hint/m.HintHigh (the confirming heartbeat context) for every returned status rather than s.ctx. A follower copies the received context into ReadyToRead, and pendingReadIndex.addReady updates only a batch with that exact SystemCtx. Thus a prefix containing distinct remote contexts has a sourced producer/consumer mismatch worth testing. No legal multi-request execution has yet been constructed, and no client timeout or consensus-safety violation has been measured.\\n\\nProtection/counterevidence: batch completion remains gated by applied index; context generation chooses a nonzero random Low; duplicate same-context admission at the leader is ignored; unknown response senders are filtered at Peer.Handle; term transitions discard pending read support. Periodic heartbeat broadcasts target the latest pending read context, suggesting prefix release is an intended path rather than requiring arbitrary per-link response reordering. This needs a legal schedule and an exact identity-preservation obligation, not an invented global eventual-completion deadline. Source inspection also explains the initial apparent missing sender-membership check in candidate vote counting: the caller Peer.Handle provides it. Engine processing persists Raft state before ordinary post-save update handling, with explicitly separated early replication/fast-apply paths; their complete contract remains to be read.\",\n        \"remaining\": [\n          \"Complete the core overview by tracing configuration application/rejection, persisted reconstruction and application completion through their actual owners and caller duties; do not label the current partial understanding ready.\",\n          \"For the remote read prefix-release lead, establish that two distinct forwarded contexts can coexist and a later heartbeat confirmation can legally release both under the production transport/caller ordering. Inspect existing tests and the relevant interface contract before selecting a fixed check.\",\n          \"Determine whether another result producer or retry mechanism can deliver the earlier context after prefix release, and distinguish a local correlation obligation from eventual client completion or timeout behavior.\",\n          \"Retain recovery and membership-transition variants as unread details; the observed guards do not establish their correctness.\"\n        ],\n        \"understanding\": \"updated\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The read queue and remote result path disagree about the identity emitted for earlier confirmed requests, while the consumer requires exact identity. Contract/history work is cheaper and more discriminating than an immediate broad cluster experiment. The initial core explanation is intentionally partial; no Candidate or accepted map basis is fabricated.\",\n       